#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg-2.rst:107
#: 255efce93c094bf8a46198a78839888f
msgid "Subkeys vs primary keys"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg-2.rst:109
#: 8a32d98d2a274137aeb9ed8219f5f92e
msgid ""
"Split GPG-2 only knows a hash of the data being signed. Therefore, it cannot "
"differentiate between e.g. signatures of a piece of data or signatures of "
"another key. This means that a client can use Split GPG-2 to sign other "
"keys, which :doc:`split-gpg` did not allow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/security-in-qubes/split-gpg-2.rst:111
#: a21fa86314e4484786477398e2c55db4
msgid ""
"To prevent this, Split GPG-2 creates a new GnuPG home directory and imports "
"the secret subkeys (**not** the primary key!) to it. Clients will be able to "
"use the secret parts of the subkeys, but not of the primary key. If your "
"primary key is able to sign data and certify other keys, and your only "
"subkey can only perform encryption, this means that all signing will fail. "
"To make signing work again, generate a subkey that is capable of signing but "
"**not** certification. Split GPG-2 does not generate this key for you, so "
"you need to generate it yourself. If you want to generate a key in software, "
"use the ``addkey`` command of ``gpg2 --edit-key``. If you want to generate a "
"key on a smartcard or other hardware token, use ``addcardkey`` instead. You "
"will need to import your public keys again."
msgstr ""

